====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|now|rare}} [[opportunity|Opportunity]] or [[scope]] (to do something). {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Acts|I|text=Thou lorde whiche knowest the hertes of all men, shewe whether thou hast chosen of these two, that the one maye take the '''roume''' of this ministracion, and apostleshippe from the which Judas by transgression fell, that he myght goo to his awne place.}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|text=Nor shalt thou give me '''room''' to doubt whether it be necessity or love, that inspires this condescending impulse.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Space ''for'' something, or ''to'' carry out an activity. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jonathan Franklin|journal=The Guardian|date=27 Aug 2010
|passage=He explains they have enough '''room''' to stand and lie down, points out the "little cup to brush our teeth", and the place where they pray.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A particular [[portion]] of [[space]]. {{defdate|from 11th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1614|author=w:Thomas Overbury|title=Characters
|passage=If he have but twelve pence in his purse, he will give it for the best '''room''' in a playhouse.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|14|8|passage=When thou art bidden of any man to a wedding, sit not down in the highest '''room'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|figuratively}} Sufficient space {{m|en|for}} or {{m|en|to}} ''do'' something. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Freeholder|issue=21|date=March 2, 1716|passage=There was no prince in the empire who had '''room''' for such an alliance.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Roger Bootle|journal=The Telegraph|date=12 Sep 2010
|passage=There are major disagreements within the Coalition and politicians always want to retain '''room''' for manoeuvre.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[space]] between the timbers of a ship's frame. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[place|Place]]; [[stead]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1749|author=Henry Fielding|title=The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling|passage=For this purpose I have shown that no acquisitions of guilt can compensate the loss of that solid inward comfort of mind, which is the sure companion of innocence and virtue; nor can in the least balance the evil of that horror and anxiety which, in their '''room''', guilt introduces into our bosoms.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1900|author=James George Frazer|title=The Golden Bough|edition=2nd|volume=2|page=37|text=A ram was accepted as a vicarious sacrifice '''in room of''' the royal victim.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A separate part of a building, enclosed by walls, a floor and a ceiling. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|text=Miss Bingley made no answer, and soon afterwards she got up and walked about the '''room'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=10|passage=He looked round the poor '''room''', at the distempered walls, and the bad engravings in meretricious frames, the crinkly paper and wax flowers on the chiffonier; and he thought of a '''room''' like Father Bryan's, with panelling, with cut glass, with tulips in silver pots, such a '''room''' as he had hoped to have for his own.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|with possessive pronoun}} (One's) [[bedroom]].
#: {{ux|en|Go to your '''room'''!}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=6|passage=She was so mad she wouldn't speak to me for quite a spell, but at last I coaxed her into going up to Miss Emmeline's '''room''' and fetching down a tintype of the missing Deacon man.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=20|passage=&lsquo;No. I only opened the door a foot and put my head in. The street lamps shine into that '''room'''. I could see him. He was all right. Sleeping like a great grampus. Poor, poor chap.’}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} A set of rooms inhabited by someone; one's [[lodgings]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|_|in the singular|metonymy}} The people in a room. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''room''' was on its feet.}}
#: ''He was good at reading '''rooms'''.''
#: ''It was fun to watch her work the '''room'''.''
# {{lb|en|mining}} An area for working in a [[coal mine]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|caving}} A portion of a cave that is wider than a [[passage]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|Internet|countable}} An [[IRC]] or [[chat room]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Some users may not be able to access the AOL '''room'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title=My Internet Girl|year=2000|artist=w:Aaron Carter|passage=Here in the '''room''' we are as one / Together you and me, together you and me / Hours connect / As we switch on}}
# Place or position in [[society]]; [[office]]; [[rank]]; [[post]], sometimes when [[vacated]] by its former [[occupant]].
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=iii|page=34|passage=When ſhee that rules in ''Rhamnis'' golden gates, {{...}}<br>Shall make me ſolely Emperour of ''Aſia'',<br>Then ſhall your meeds and vallours be aduaunſt<br>To '''roomes''' of honour and Nobilitie.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|act=III|scene=ii|passage=Let Bianca take her sister's '''room'''.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|2|22|text=When he heard that Archelaus did reign in Judea in the '''room''' of his father Herod.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1848|editor=w:Henry Walter|author=w:William Tyndale|title=Doctrinal Treatises and Introductions to Different Portions of the Holy Scriptures
|passage=Neither that I look for a higher '''room''' in heaven.}}
# A quantity of [[furniture]] sufficient to furnish one room.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1985|author=w:August Wilson|title={{w|Fences (play)|Fences}}
|passage=“I understand you need some furniture and can’t get no credit.” I liked to fell over. He say, “I’ll give you all the credit you want, but you got to pay the interest on it.” I told him, “Give me three '''rooms''' worth and charge whatever you want.”}}

